THOUGHT: `curr.next` is annotated @Nullable, so NullAway flags `n.prev = null`. Logically, when `c == head` and size > 0 after removal, `n` must be non-null (there was at least one other element). To satisfy NullAway without changing behavior, I’ll add a local non-null assertion via a check and early return if `n` is null (defensive, though unreachable), and similarly guard the later `n.prev = p` to avoid new errors. Then I’ll run the compile to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '146,170s/if (c == head) {/if (c == head) {
      if (n == null) {
        head = null;
        tail = p;
        return;
      }/' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/PooledLinkedList.java && sed -i '166,170s/p.next = n;/p.next = n;
    if (n == null) return;/' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/PooledLinkedList.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway:.*errors" new_build_output.log || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log
```

